\DOC BIGNUM_DIGITIZE_TAC

\TYPE {BIGNUM_DIGITIZE_TAC : string -> term -> tactic}

\SYNOPSIS
Given a multi-word memory read term, splits that bignum into individual 64-bit
digit variables, adding one assumption per digit and rewriting the bignum's
value as a weighted sum of those digits.

\DESCRIBE
{s : string} is the prefix used to name the new digit variables, formed by
appending an index ({0}, {1}, ...) to it; {tm : term} is the multi-word
memory-read term to digitize, specifying both the base address and the number
{n} of 64-bit digits. For a goal {A ?- t} where {tm} reads {n} words from some
base address in some state, the tactic introduces fresh variables named from
the prefix with indices {0 ... n-1} and adds one equation per digit, each
stating that the corresponding single-word read at the base address offset by a
multiple of 8 equals that variable. If an assumption of {A} (or a subterm of
the conclusion) states that {tm} equals some term, that assumption is replaced
by (or that occurrence rewritten to) the equation that the weighted sum of the
digits' values with powers of two to the 64 equals that term; when {tm} is not
mentioned in this way, only the digit assumptions appear and the rest of {A}
and {t} are unchanged.
{
             A ?- t
   ===========================================  BIGNUM_DIGITIZE_TAC s tm
   A', x1 = d_0, ..., xn = d_n-1 ?- t'
}
\noindent Here {A'} and {t'} differ from {A} and {t} only by the above
substitution, possibly nowhere.

\FAILURE
In the observed traces, failure occurs when the term argument is not a
multi-word read of the expected shape: a bare state variable (not a combination
at all), or a single-word read whose size cannot be read off as a numeral. In
both cases the failure arises inside the tactic while decomposing the term
argument.

\COMMENTS
The observed traces only ever show this one kind of transformation, always on
goals about machine-state reads.

\ENDDOC
